{"id":"[REDACTED]","object":"chat.completion","created":1788223382,"model":"mercury-2","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"**CONCRETE_BREAK_FOUND**\n\n**Evidence**\n\n1. **Duplicate key in the test expectation** – `test/ecosystem-eval-profile.test.js` contains two `STATE_FILE` entries in the expected object (lines 190‑199). The first expects `'data/state.json'` while the second (line 198) overwrites it with `'data/state-paper.json'`【open_file†L190-L199】. The earlier expectation contradicts the actual config (see 2) but is silently masked by the later key, so the test can pass even when the config is wrong.\n\n2. **Config sets `STATE_FILE` to the paper path** – `ecosystem.config.js` defines `STATE_FILE` for the `ogz‑prime‑v2` app as `'data/state-paper.json'` (line 95)【open_file†L95-L96】. The test’s first expectation (`'data/state.json'`) does not match this value.\n\n3. **The test suite was never executed** – Running the test file with `npx jest test/ecosystem-eval-profile.test.js --runInBand` failed with exit code 127 because `jest` is not available in the execution environment【run_check†L1-L4】. Therefore the “pass” reported by the earlier hand‑rolled script is not proof that the test actually succeeds.\n\n4. **Operator‑environment keys are present (even if undefined)** – Loading the PM2 config with `NODE_ENV='test'` (which skips the `.env` load) and checking `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(appEnv, key)` for every `OPERATOR_ENV_KEYS` entry shows that each key exists on the `env` object (all `true`)【run_check†L1-L34】. This proves the test’s `toHaveProperty(key, undefined)` check is meaningful: the keys are present with the value `undefined`.\n\n5. **PM2 behavior** – PM2 receives the `env` object as‑is. Since the keys are present (see 4) but have the value `undefined`, PM2 will forward them as environment variables with the literal string `\"undefined\"` (Node’s `process.env` only accepts strings). This matches the test’s expectation of keys being present with an undefined value, but it also means that any consumer expecting a real value will see the string `\"undefined\"`.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nThe duplicate `STATE_FILE` entry in the test masks a mismatch with the real configuration, and the test suite was never actually run to catch this. This constitutes a concrete break in the fix’s validation. The original “NO_CONCRETE_BREAK_FOUND” verdict must be changed to **CONCRETE_BREAK_FOUND**.","tool_calls":[],"tool_call_id":null},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":20461,"completion_tokens":911,"total_tokens":21372,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":355}},"warning":null,"reasoning_summary":null}